[Themes "development-lead,f-pawn-tension"]
[Kind "sideline"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d6 { A quiet reply: Black guards e5 and keeps the position closed. White does not need to force anything. }
4. Nf3 { Develops and keeps all the options open: White can still take on e5 or let Black take on f4. } 4... Be7 (4... exf4 5. d4 { Takes the centre first; the f4 pawn can be won back later with Bxf4. } 5... Nh5 6. g3 { Opens the way for the bishop to take the pawn on f4 or the queen to attack it. } 6... Be7 7. Qd3) (4... Nc6 5. Bb5 { Pins the knight and lines up castling; the bishop also takes some pressure off the centre. } 5... exf4 6. d4 a6 7. Be2) (4... Bg4 5. Be2 { Develops and prepares to castle. If Black takes on f3 the bishop recaptures. } 5... Nc6 6. O-O)
5. Bc4 { Develops the bishop on the diagonal aimed at the f7 pawn. } 5... O-O (5... exf4 6. d3 { Supports the e4 pawn so that ...Nxe4 is not a threat, and clears the bishop on c1. } 6... Nh5 7. O-O O-O)
6. d3 { Supports the e4 pawn so that ...Nxe4 is not a threat, and opens the bishop on c1. } 6... Nc6 7. O-O { Castles. The king is safe and the rook sits behind the f-pawn. } 7... exf4 (7... Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3) 8. Bxf4 { Wins the pawn back with the bishop developing at the same time. Plan: Qd2 or Qe1, then Rae1 and a central push with e5 when it is safe. } *
